# SEO-05. Served by Cloudflare Workers Static Assets, which reads this file out of the
# assets directory — dist/client, per the adapter-generated dist/server/wrangler.json
# ("assets": {"directory": "../client"}). Astro copies public/ into that directory verbatim.
#
# WHY THIS FILE AND NOT astro.config's `redirects` KEY — AND WHY THE PLAN'S REASON IS WRONG
# Plan 05-13 says the config key "emits an HTML page carrying a meta http-equiv refresh" and that
# SEO-05 rejects it. That is true of a static build with NO adapter. MEASURED here, with
# adapter: cloudflare(): the config key emits NO HTML page at all — @astrojs/cloudflare compiles it
# into this very file, writing BOTH /portfolio/ and /portfolio as real 301s, and a request answers
#     HTTP/1.1 301  location: /photos  ·  0-byte body
# exactly as the rules below do. So the plan's "assert no dist/client/portfolio/ document was
# emitted" check CANNOT distinguish the two mechanisms in this repository; it is true and vacuous,
# and the suite says so rather than counting it as evidence.
#
# The config key is nonetheless not what ships, for a reason that survives being measured: it is
# invisible. A redirect is a routing decision served to every visitor (threat T-05-13-01) and it
# should be reviewable where a reader looks for it, with its reasoning beside it — which is here.
#
# MEASURED, and it is why the suite asserts this file byte-for-byte against dist/client/_redirects:
# with BOTH mechanisms present the adapter APPENDS its rules to this file's copy, silently
# producing four rules where two were written, two of them duplicates, with no warning. A byte
# comparison is the only check that notices a second, unreviewed source of redirects appearing.
#
# TWO LITERAL RULES, NO WILDCARD, NO CAPTURE (threat T-05-13-01)
# `/portfolio/*  /photos/:splat  301` is the construct that turns a redirects file into an
# open redirect, and it would also invent sub-paths that never existed. MEASURED on the
# legacy branch: `git ls-tree -r --name-only legacy/nextjs-portfolio` under src/app/portfolio
# contains exactly layout.tsx and page.tsx — no nested segment, no [slug], and the category
# filter was React useState rather than a route. So /portfolio was one page and one page only.
#
# THE SECOND RULE IS NOT SYMMETRY, IT IS A MEASUREMENT
# With only the unslashed rule present, MEASURED against real workerd:
#     GET /portfolio   -> 301  location: /photos
#     GET /portfolio/  -> 404
# A rule does not match its own trailing-slash form. Every bookmark and inbound link ending
# in /portfolio/ would have 404'd, which is the whole failure SEO-05 exists to prevent, and
# no build error catches it. Both forms are asserted in test/public/seo.node.test.ts.
#
# Query strings are carried across by Cloudflare without being written here — MEASURED:
# /portfolio?cat=nature&x=1 -> location: /photos?cat=nature&x=1
/portfolio   /photos  301
/portfolio/  /photos  301
